### Multi-phase or multi-PR plan

**You own the plan, not the code.** The plan is a checklist an owner runs box by box and the operator audits from the evidence. Do not implement.

Host mapping is in `.cursor/rules/rigor-mode.mdc`. Explorers are `generalPurpose` or `explore`. This repo has no `scripts/check-plan.mjs`. The check in step 6 is manual.

1. When the change is one or two files with an obvious approach, skip the plan. Say so and stop. Done when either `skip: obvious` is the reply, or the work is large enough to plan.
2. Settle open questions by prototype before you write. Run `.cursor/rules/rigor-playbook-prototype.mdc` for each. Keep the branch, the SHA, and the screenshots for Appendix A. Ask the operator only about a product or preference call that no run can settle. Done when every open question has prototype evidence or is labeled unproven.
3. Explore in dispatched workers. Each returns file pointers, conventions, test commands, and entry points. No inlined dumps. Done when those pointers exist for every touched subsystem.
4. Copy the skeleton below into the plan file and fill every placeholder. Unless the operator names a path, write the file under the agent store's `docs/` (`/cursor/stores/self/docs/` on a Cursor cloud agent, otherwise `/tmp/<slug>-plan.md`). Do not put the plan in the product tree unless the operator asked for it there. Keep every heading and every sub-block in the order shown. One section per PR. Name the execution playbook in **How to read this**. Pick between Autopilot-full and Autopilot-stack per the rule at the end of `.cursor/rules/rigor-playbook-autopilot-stack.mdc`. A standing program takes Orchestrate. Done when the plan file exists with every placeholder filled or explicitly marked n/a.
5. Write the body as a how-to. Appendices hold explanation and reference. Follow Writing the reply. No long dashes. No mid-sentence colons. Done when the body follows that voice.
6. Check the plan yourself. Every skeleton heading is present, in order. Every placeholder is filled or marked n/a. Every PR section has Depends on, Files, Build, You see, Verify unit, Verify live (ten lanes, lane 1 is regression against trunk), Verify perf, Review gate, and Merge. The verification sentence opens each verification block. Write the findings down and fix every miss. Done when that check lists zero misses.
7. Hand back. Post the plan path and the check output, then stop. Execution starts on the operator's explicit go. Done when the path and the check output are in the reply.

**Verification.** Tests alone are not sufficient verification. A PR is verified only when its unit, live, and perf boxes are all checked. That sentence is the verification rule. Every verification block opens with it. The live block is mandatory. Ten lanes at the PR head drive the real surface. Each lane is one box with a concrete scenario, the screenshot it saves, and its pass predicate. One lane is the regression lane against trunk. The perf gate is dual-sided. Trunk and head must both produce the named metric. A PR that changes an interaction is review-gated.

**Surface driver.** Pick it by surface. Browser and web UI use the host browser tools. CLI and TUI use a PTY or the project binary. A PR that touches two surfaces gets lanes on both. A surface with no driver is a risk in Appendix C, and its live block still names how each lane drives it.

